From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 16:01:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Reuse the glyphs of words seen before

Long documents shape the same words thousands of times, and shaping was
most of the time a first layout spent in parley: on the HTML Standard it
took 1.4 s of a 3.8 s layout.

When a font's shaping cannot act across a space, a run's glyphs are its
words' and spaces' glyphs laid end to end. The shaper now checks each font
once: no GSUB lookup may read the space, and GPOS may read it only as the
glyph right before or after the glyphs it moves, which are then listed as
space-sensitive. Runs in left-to-right Latin, Greek, Cyrillic or common
text are split at spaces, keeping graphemes whole; pieces seen before take
their cached glyphs, and the rest are shaped together with one neighbouring
piece on each side, so a space-sensitive glyph still sees its space, then
remembered unless they begin or end with a space-sensitive glyph.

Kerning, ligatures and marks within a word are shaped as before. The cache
holds up to 2048 words for each of 8 font and option sets.
